    <lineage>
      <method>
        <methtype>field</methtype>
        <methdesc>We captured flightless and recently fledged juvenile female and male mallards during 2–30 August 2018, and 16 July–30 August 2019 using multiple methods, including nightlighting, drive trapping, using baited swim-in traps, and rocket-netting. Captured birds were immediately transported to a mobile surgical facility for intracoelomic implantation of a 32 g GPS/GSM transmitter with a percutaneous antenna. All surgeries were performed by licensed wildlife veterinarians, who had experience and training in the implantation procedure. We programmed transmitters to collect 5–8 GPS locations per day while birds were north of 45°N latitude as part of an assessment of post-breeding habitat use (Anchor 2022), and locations every 1–2 days in areas south of 45°N. Our research was approved by the Institutional Animal Care and Use Committee at South Dakota State University (18-048A).</methdesc>
      </method>
